    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Hi, I am Kyle Reddoch</h2>

    <p class="mb-4">I work in that weird overlap where managed services, cybersecurity, and general tech chaos all meet. Most days I am helping small and midsize businesses stay online, stay secure, and understand what the logs are trying to tell us instead of ignoring them until something catches fire.</p>

    <p class="mb-8">Online you will usually see me as <strong>CybersecKyle</strong>, writing about security, MSP life, Eleventy, and whatever tools are currently making my workflow less painful or more fun.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">What I Do</h2>

    <p class="mb-4">My day job is in <strong>managed services and cybersecurity</strong>. That usually means:</p>

    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>Designing and tuning <strong>RMM and EDR</strong> stacks</li>
      <li>Hardening endpoints and networks for real world small business constraints</li>
      <li>Building and refining <strong>playbooks</strong> for incident response, patching, and onboarding</li>
      <li>Translating security jargon into language that owners and staff actually understand</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mb-8">I enjoy pulling messy environments into something structured and repeatable. Getting a client from “nobody knows how this works” to “we have diagrams, runbooks, and alerts that make sense” is extremely satisfying.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Things I Am Building And Writing</h2>

    <p class="mb-4">I like shipping things that other people can actually use, not just talk about.</p>

    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>A personal blog at <a href="https://www.kylereddoch.me">kylereddoch.me</a> where I write about security, MSP operations, Eleventy, tooling, and the realities of doing this work.</li>
      <li>Eleventy projects and plugins, including starters and small quality of life helpers for content driven sites.</li>
      <li>Security templates and how-to guides that aim to make good practices feel reachable instead of overwhelming.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mb-8">I care a lot about <strong>practical security</strong>. Less buzzword bingo, more “can a small team maintain this without hating life in six months”.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">How I Got Here</h2>

    <p class="mb-4">I did not wake up one day and decide to be “in cybersecurity.” I got here by:</p>

    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>Fixing everyday IT problems and noticing how often the root cause was something security related</li>
      <li>Seeing how fragile some small business systems really were</li>
      <li>Realizing that most users are not “the problem,” they simply have not been given sane tools or clear guidance</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mb-8">Over time I leaned harder into <strong>blue team work</strong>, threat detection, and building defenses that fit actual business workflows. I like breaking things enough to understand them, then using that knowledge to keep them running and resilient.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">What You Will Find On This Site</h2>

    <p class="mb-4">This site is where I connect all of that:</p>

    <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>Security and privacy breakdowns for normal people and busy admins</li>
      <li>MSP and IT operations lessons, including the parts that do not make it into vendor webinars</li>
      <li>Hands-on Eleventy and web tooling write-ups</li>
      <li>Occasional posts about <strong>Apple gear, homelab tinkering</strong>, F1, and music because humans are allowed to have hobbies</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote class="mb-8">
      <p>If you come away from a post with one concrete thing you can try at work tomorrow, then it did its job.</p>
    </blockquote>

